
**JDBC vs Hibernate**
So JDBC is pretty low-level — you're writing raw SQL yourself, managing connections yourself, and when you get data back from the database you have to 
manually pull each column out of the ResultSet and map it to your Java object. It gives you full control but there's a lot of repetitive boilerplate.
Hibernate is an ORM that sits on top of JDBC and handles all of that for you — the SQL generation, the connection management, the object mapping. I 
actually did this conversion in my project, going from Spring Data JPA down to raw JDBC and then to direct Hibernate, so I really saw the difference 
firsthand. With JDBC I had to write a RowMapper and manually do rs.getString("first_name") for every field. With Hibernate you just put @Entity on the 
class and it figures the rest out.

**Statement vs PreparedStatement vs CallableStatement**
Statement is the basic one where you just concatenate your SQL as a string — it works but it's dangerous because if user input gets into that string you're
wide open to SQL injection, and it's also slower because the database has to parse it every time. PreparedStatement is what you actually want to use — you 
write the SQL with question mark placeholders, the database compiles it once, and then you just plug in the values each time you run it. That's both faster 
and safe. CallableStatement is specifically for stored procedures. In my JDBC implementation I used PreparedStatement for all the inserts and updates, and
I also used a KeyHolder with it to capture the auto-generated ID that came back after an INSERT — that part was a bit tricky but it worked well.

**How to prevent SQL injection**
The number one thing is just never concatenate user input directly into a SQL string — that's where the vulnerability comes from. You use PreparedStatement
with parameterized queries so the database always treats the input as data, never as SQL to execute. If you're using Hibernate or any ORM, it handles the 
parameterization for you by default which is one of the nice safety benefits. Beyond that you want to validate inputs on the application side, apply 
least-privilege database permissions so even if something goes wrong the damage is contained, and never expose detailed database error messages to the end 
user because those can give attackers useful information.

**What is ORM**
ORM stands for Object-Relational Mapping. The basic problem is that relational databases think in tables and rows, and Java thinks in objects and classes —
those two worlds don't naturally talk to each other. ORM is the bridge. You annotate your Java classes with things like @Entity and @Column, and the ORM 
framework takes care of translating between your objects and the database rows automatically. So instead of writing INSERT INTO employees... you just call
save() and it handles it. Hibernate is the most popular ORM in Java, and JPA is the standard specification that defines what an ORM in Java should look like.

**JPA vs Hibernate**
JPA is a specification — it defines the rules and the interfaces for how ORM should work in Java, but it doesn't actually do anything by itself. Hibernate 
is the implementation that actually runs. A good way to think about it is JPA is like an interface in Java, and Hibernate is the class that implements it. 
When I write @Entity or use EntityManager I'm coding against the JPA standard, but Hibernate is what's actually executing under the hood. Then Spring Data 
JPA adds one more layer on top — it gives you JpaRepository so you don't even have to write EntityManager calls, you just call findAll() or save() and it's
done.

**Persistent States in Entity Lifecycle**
There are four states and I actually think about them a lot when debugging Hibernate issues. Transient is when you've just done new Employee() — Hibernate
doesn't know it exists, it's not tracked, it's not in the database. Persistent is when the entity is associated with an open session — this is the important
one because Hibernate is watching it, and if you change any field it will automatically detect that and sync it to the database when the transaction commits,
that's dirty checking. Detached is after the session closes — the object still has its data and its ID but Hibernate is no longer watching it. If you try to 
use it in a new session you need to call merge() first. Removed means you called remove() on it — it's still in memory for now but it's scheduled to be 
deleted when the transaction commits.

**Mapping Relationships**
@OneToOne is simple — one record maps to exactly one other, like a user and their profile. @OneToMany and @ManyToOne are probably the most common — like one 
department having many employees. You always put @ManyToOne on the many side because that's where the foreign key column actually lives in the database. 
@ManyToMany is where it gets more interesting — I built this in my Hibernate project with students and courses. A student can enroll in many courses, and 
a course can have many students. Hibernate handles this by creating a join table in the middle, in my case students_courses with student_id and course_id.
You define the join table on the owning side with @JoinTable, and on the other side you just write mappedBy to point back to the owning side.

**Cascade Types**
Cascade basically means "when I do something to the parent, automatically do the same thing to the children." PERSIST means save the parent, save the 
children too. MERGE means merge the parent, merge the children. REMOVE means delete the parent, delete all the children — you want to be careful with that 
one. ALL does everything. I actually ran into a real bug with this in my project. I had CascadeType.ALL on my @ManyToMany, and when two students shared the 
same course object, saving the second student caused Hibernate to try to persist a course that was already saved — it threw a detached entity exception. 
I ended up removing cascade from the relationship entirely and saving courses independently first, which fixed it.

**Fetch Types**
Fetch type controls when Hibernate actually goes to the database to load the related data. LAZY means it waits — it only loads the collection when you 
actually touch it in code, and it's the default for collections. EAGER means it loads everything immediately with a JOIN when it loads the parent. LAZY 
is generally better for performance, but there's a catch — if the session is already closed when you try to access the lazy collection, you get a 
LazyInitializationException. I hit this exact issue in my Many-to-Many project. After findAll() the session closed, and when I looped through 
student.getCourses() it blew up. I switched to EAGER to fix it quickly, but in a real production app you'd handle it with @Transactional or a specific fetch
join query so you're not loading everything unnecessarily.

**First and Second Level Cache*
The first-level cache is built into every Hibernate Session and you don't have to do anything to enable it — it's always there. Within one session, if you 
load the same entity by ID twice, Hibernate just returns the cached version the second time without hitting the database. It gets cleared automatically when
the session closes. The second-level cache is optional and shared across all sessions — it lives at the SessionFactory level so it survives after individual
transactions end. You have to explicitly configure it and pick a cache provider like Ehcache. It's most useful for reference data that's read a lot but 
rarely changes, like lookup tables or configuration data — things where going to the database every time is wasteful.

**Left Join vs Right Join vs Inner Join vs Full Outer Join vs Cross Join**
INNER JOIN is the strictest — you only get rows where there's a match in both tables. No match, no row. LEFT JOIN gives you everything from the left table
regardless, and where there's no match on the right side you just get NULLs. So if I join employees to departments with a left join, I get all employees
including any who don't have a department yet. RIGHT JOIN is the mirror of that — all rows from the right table, NULLs where there's no match on the left. 
FULL OUTER JOIN combines both sides — you get everything from both tables, with NULLs wherever there's no corresponding match. CROSS JOIN is the cartesian 
product — every row from the left paired with every row from the right, no condition needed. It's not something you use often in practice but it comes up 
if you need to generate all possible combinations of two sets.

**UNION vs UNION ALL**
Both stack the results of two SELECT queries on top of each other into one result set, and both require the same number of columns with compatible types.
The difference is UNION removes duplicates — it does an internal sort and deduplication, which takes extra processing time. UNION ALL just gives you 
everything as-is including duplicates, and because it skips that dedup step it's faster. My default is to reach for UNION ALL unless I have a specific 
reason to remove duplicates, because at any meaningful scale the performance difference is real and most of the time if you're writing the queries correctly
duplicates shouldn't show up anyway.



